# core.autocrlf is true on this machine, so a fresh clone would hand these files
# to sh.exe with CRLF endings. A shell script whose shebang ends in \r does not
# run, and a make variable that ends in \r never compares equal to what it is
# tested against - which would silently disable the contract check this build
# depends on. Both files stay LF everywhere.
*.sh            text eol=lf
makefile.init   text eol=lf

# contracts/ is verified byte for byte by tools/check_contracts.sh, and that
# hash is taken over the bytes on disk. With core.autocrlf=true and nothing
# pinning these files, a checkout writes CRLF and every hash moves at once: the
# build stops on the first "make" of a fresh clone and reports that the contract
# changed, when the content is identical to the byte.
#
# That is worse than an inconvenience. The one thing this mechanism asks of
# people is that they never run --update just to clear a red build, and a check
# that fires when nothing is wrong is precisely how that rule gets broken. Pin
# these to LF so that a moved hash means what it claims to mean.
#
# (VF_Vibration_Module hit exactly this - its Phase 2 report, section on the
# first headless build after a fresh checkout. Same file as VF_Angle_Module.)
contracts/*.md          text eol=lf
contracts/*.dbc         text eol=lf
contracts/SHA256SUMS    text eol=lf
